= Overfitting =

== Overview ==
'''Overfitting''' occurs when a learning procedure adapts too strongly to finite-sample details, noise, artifacts, or the development process so that apparent fit is better than performance on genuinely unseen target data.

Overfitting is about generalization, not model size alone.

== Training and evaluation ==
A typical overfit model has lower training error than test error.

However, the size of the gap depends on the metric, data, model, regularization, and evaluation process.

Repeated tuning on a validation set can overfit that validation set even when model parameters are never directly trained on it.

== Model capacity ==
High model capacity can increase the ability to fit noise, but:

<pre>
large model ≠ overfit model
</pre>

Modern overparameterized models can interpolate training data and still generalize well.

This is one reason the classical single U-shaped complexity story is not universal.

== Memorization ==
Memorization can contribute to overfitting, but it is not identical to overfitting.

A model can memorize rare or exceptional examples and still generalize useful structure to new data.

== Prevention and diagnosis ==
Common tools include:

* held-out validation;
* cross-validation;
* regularization;
* early stopping;
* data augmentation;
* larger or better-matched datasets;
* leakage checks;
* independent testing.

No single method guarantees generalization.

== Related concepts ==
[[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Validation set]], [[Test set]], [[Bias–variance tradeoff]], [[Model selection bias]], and [[Data leakage (machine learning)]] distinguish different forms of apparent performance failure.

== Development can overfit without new parameter training ==
Overfitting can occur at more than one level. In ordinary parameter fitting, a model may adapt to noise in its training observations. In hyperparameter search, the choice among many models can adapt to chance fluctuations in validation results. If the final test data are used repeatedly to choose prompts, checkpoints or architectures, the supposed test results also become part of the development process.

== Appropriate diagnosis ==
Compare training, validation and independent test performance using the same relevant metric, but state the sampling and population assumptions. A low training error with a larger test error can suggest overfitting; a high error on both sets can instead indicate [[Related to::Underfitting|underfitting]]. A small gap does not prove success when the target task or sample is unrepresentative.

== Limits of remedies ==
Regularization, early stopping, cross-validation and data augmentation may help with particular overfitting mechanisms. They do not repair contaminated labels, invalid group splits or an evaluation population unrelated to deployment. Generalization must be supported by new, appropriately independent evidence.

